WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.380
You've been able to play incredible roles.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:31.060
And you've played fun roles and a lot of, should we say a
bit shy, bad guys.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.260
I really like bad guys!

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.900
No one wants to do prince charming, everyone
wants to do the villain.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.980
But what is it with you that allows for you
to get these parts, what do you yourself think

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.340
it is about you appearance that makes you
get them?

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:49.940
Three things: Ha-ha!

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:52.740
Timing.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.640
When started in the beginning of the 80s,
everyone was protesting outside

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.940
the movie production companies.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.640
That they weren't portraying minorities.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:14.080
Like latinos, black people, arabs or asian people, they
were always playing the bad guys.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.300
They were always crooks.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.020
So in the end, Hollywood gave up and said: «Let's
go back to doing Russians and Germans».

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.680
Because they'll never protest!

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.520
As they did in the 40s, 50s and 60s.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.020
So the timing was good.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.520
So a producer ... and producers are so stupid!

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:39.300
They could call and you could get a question
like: «I'm sure you can speak Hungarian, right?»

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.820
«Yes!» I said. «That's no problem!»

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.940
You know, there's one line in Hungarian.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.860
Those of you who've seen American movies.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.740
Some star enters and says something like ... *Attempts
to speak Hungarian.*

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:01.840
And then the other one  says: *«Hungarian»* Before someone says: «Let's continue in English.»

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.160
And then they continue the conversation in
English, it's a little twisted.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.060
But that's how you do movies here.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:14.520
So I like doing a little ... I come from the
theatre, and there you do a lot of strange characters.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:18.380
Whereas in your private life, you try to be
normal.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.780
And it's always a good time.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.700
I'm thinking about accents ... If you were
to ...

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:32.400
It's interesting how Americans think that Europeans, in general, can be so extremely horrible.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:32.900
Mhm.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.460
If you, right now, were to ... I just want
to see how I would feel ...

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.760
If you now threatened me ...

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.560
In a couple of different accents, European ones.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:49.520
Just ...

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:51.600
OK, I'll do two.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.460
Yes, do two, a little tough.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:54.360
Yes.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:03:01.400
I will do one ... With a Norwegian accent,
he's not gonna be scared.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.180
«Hello!»

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.880
«Nice looking guy!»

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.480
«I think I'm gonna cut off your balls!»

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.560
«And then eat your brain!»

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.260
It feels ... It's just pleasant!

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.680
It's just a pleasant little chat about ...

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.100
But if you go further east ...

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.500
«Ey, you good looking guy!»

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.820
«I'm gonna rip off your balls, then I think
I'm gonna eat your brains.»

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.920
Please don't.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:04:02.680
I've read that you've said that you're four
people in this elite club who get these parts ...

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.400
... the really good villain parts in Hollywood.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:12.840
And you've mentioned ... You've mentioned
Malkovich, you've mentioned Dafoe ...

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.480
... and you've mentioned Christopher Walken.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:17.480
Yes.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.020
And I thought that we'll ... Get it in ... I
thought that we could do the villain top list.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.480
Now we'll decide, we'll just rank them! And you yourself will rank ...

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:33.200
So here we've got Willem Dafoe, John Malkovich, Christopher Walken and Peter Stormare.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.700
And I don't think is the final ranking according
to you.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.780
I just have to check that he's not in here.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.380
OK.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:49.800
So ...

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:55.120
In a shared second place, a little below ...

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.240
There's still a little Scandinavian modesty left in you?

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.660
No, there isn't!

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:06.920
Right.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.900
But here we've got Christopher Walken as a
Bond villain.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:17.340
Have you never wanted that part?

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.360
I turned it down once.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.040
You did?

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:24.700
Can I say something?

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:25.380
Yes.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.060
Now it's not an option anymore.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.180
But ...

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.100
I've never understood James Bond!

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.980
I think it's the lamest thing ever!

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:37.480
Excuse me!

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.400
Is that why you turned it down?

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.160
I probably had other offers that were better ...

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.880
... and to to the same, you know ...

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:49.880
«Yeah mister Bond, I'm gonna kill you» ...

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:50.920
And then ...

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.500
Everyone in the audience knows, he's not gonna die, that bastard!

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.540
You don't stand a chance!

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.120
No!

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.220
To me, James Bond's formula is boring.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.980
It's ... Partly, I don't like cars.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.920
Partly, I don't like watches and fancy things.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:12.060
I have no urge to se anything that's paint
by numbers.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.440
He always survives.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.180
I'm sitting here talking about you like you're
a bad guy, but you're really a kind man?

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.280
And as I understood, you were a kind boy when
you grew up in Norrland in Sweden?

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.180
I was often alone.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.860
I often sat in the locker room with glow in
the dark colours and dreamt about moving to ...

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.760
the U.S., even when I was only five years old.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.140
And I had a premonition, I knew that I was
gonna end up living here ...

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.120
... already when I was five, as I told my parents.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.620
They said: «Are you gonna live in the U.S.,
what are you gonna do there?».

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:48.080
And I said: «Well, I don't know, but I'm
gonna live close to Disneyland and hopefully,

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:49.640
I'll be working with movies».

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.020
Your parents left your home before you did,
is that correct?

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.060
My dad was always traveling, he was an inventor.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:05.340
And I think that's why we had a few difficulties
with being in Sweden and the taxes in Sweden.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:12.340
He paid like 112 percent tax of his pay check some years, and that made him resent Sweden,

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.140
he wanted to get out, so they moved.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.700
They were quite a bit in the U.S. ...

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.920
... a lot in the U.K., a lot in Germany where they traveled around with their inventions.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.620
And at the same time, he worked for two different
companies in Sweden.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:26.180
And in the end, he just said «I've had enough
of this shit.»

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.500
«We're going to Nigeria.»

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:29.740
Nigeria?!

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.920
Nigeria.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.880
I think I was around 16 years old.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.500
And I thought «I had to finish school, so
I'll just come visit».

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.880
So they just came one day and said that you
were moving to Nigeria?

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.900
He took me to the sauna and said it.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.100
Naked and vulnerable, you were sitting there
and receiving this message?

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:50.600
Yes.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:51.880
He was a lot in the sauna, you know.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:56.720
So we were sitting in the sauna, me being
16 years old, maybe 17, I had just started high school.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.640
«I've decided that we're moving to Nigeria.»

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.400
«Nigeria?»

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:03.800
«That's quite far away.»

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.460
And it was far away in the 70s.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.200
I asked what he was gonna do there.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.760
«I'm gonna construct roads and do shit.»

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.020
«It's a really good job. And you can't work in this country anymore.»

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:18.800
«Are you joining or do you wanna finish school?»

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:25.420
I just thought «I wanna play soccer and drink
beer and meet girls!»

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.580
So I didn't think that Nigeria sounded all
that great.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:31.520
So I stayed at home and took care of our little croft.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:32.820
And they left.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.020
Could you say that the U.S. ended up being
your Nigeria?

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.540
I'm happy in this country, absolutely.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.460
But I loved growing up in a forest in Hälsingland.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.320
My hometown Arbrå.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.780
There were no dangers there.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.220
We'd just go into the woods or run around.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.760
What do you miss the most – Sweden or Norrland?

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:57.680
After so many years, you don't really miss
anything.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.060
I miss my home in Los Angeles.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.400
And then ...

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:10.520
This country is so fantastic, even in Los Angeles, you can drive for two and a half hours and you'll get to ...

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:11.540
Norrland.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:18.440
With forests and brown bears and ... Or, black
bears are the ones that are there.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.000
But it really reminds me of Norrland.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.320
Is the mentality Norrland-like as well?

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:25.300
Yes!

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:29.720
People are a bit lawless and just do what
they want.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:31.840
There aren't that many cops, you know.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:32.680
Ha-ha!

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.700
And this is where you're the most happy?

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:36.120
Yeah, it's cool.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.820
I mean, I love ordinary people.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:42.600
I'm from an environment full of very ordinary
people.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:48.660
I feel sorry for people in small places in
Sweden when I see how everything's falling apart.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.540
And I love regular people.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.300
Who are ordinary people and unpretentious.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.880
Which is why I could never live in Beverly
Hills or go to parties or doing stuff like that.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:03.660
I despise people who are very pretentious and ...

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:07.060
... who want to flaunt how much they have, or how much they earn and stuff like that.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.400
There are those who buy houses and brag about it in Los Angeles, where I live.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.440
That the house cost 47 million dollars.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.540
And they're gonna renovate for 25 million.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.180
And I think to myself «what a fucking idiot»!

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:28.200
I'm not at all impressed, I just think less
of you!

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:34.180
I read that when you were a boy, you were
a believer?

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:35.400
Yes.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:36.320
With the bible in your hand.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:37.040
Yes.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.700
Are you still, today?

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:39.820
Do you still have faith?

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:41.780
No bible!

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.580
There's no bible, but I've got this.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:46.620
This can be sufficient sometimes.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:50.780
Religious little trinklets.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:58.420
But ... The first book I ever got was probably
the bible.

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:00.000
And I'm not sure why.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:01.260
Why I got the bible.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.420
But I walked around with the bible under my
arm, and I went to our neighbors and I said ...

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.860
... that I had to bless their food.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.760
But my mother and father thought it was very
embarrassing.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:12.280
But they let me do it.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.380
Because the neighbors thought it was nice
that I stopped by and blessed them now and then.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:23.760
But ... I was born with a faith, I was born
with a faith, and it's strange, because I ...

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:34.440
... often sat by a window at home where we lived
and looked towards the hill nearby and ...

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:37.320
... it felt like I lived there.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:45.100
It was almost like I entered down there by
chance, and I said to my parents that ...

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:50.680
... I wanted to go home, that I didn't really want to be
there, «you're not really my parents».

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.840
They were probably a little scared that I
was mentally disturbed ...

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.120
But I never did anything wrong, I just sat there and daydreamed.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:06.200
And it's always been there with me, a voice
that sometimes can lead me astray ...

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.920
... but 99 percent of the time, it leads me to the right
place.

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:11.000
So, what does your spirituality look like
today?

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:14.080
My spirituality ...

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.080
Do you think there's life
after death?

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.440
Of course, there is.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.000
At least for me.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:20.600
Or else, we wouldn't be here.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.520
And go through this strange journey on earth.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:28.760
To me ... To me, it's all obvious, but it's
hard to explain.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.000
It's so obvious to me.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:35.120
If I were to try to describe it with words,
I don't think there are any word's that'll cover it.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:40.440
But if you don't believe or if you haven't
seen or experienced ... Then you might as well not believe.

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:44.680
Because it's pretty boring to live if you
don't believe.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:51.200
And sometimes they ask me «do you believe
in UFOs» or «do you believe in aliens»?

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.200
And I say «why not?».  My life is much more fun if I believe in all that.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:57.140
... than if I don't believe.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:05.040
And I've seen people ... I've seen ghosts, I've seen things, I've seen miracles in my life that are wonderful.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:08.880
And that is probably because I believe that
everything is possible.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:17.460
Peter, before we continue ...

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:22.720
I have to show a clip from you new show «Swedish Dicks».

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:27.680
And I'm saying «Swedish Dicks», and it sounds
a bit rude, but it isn't really that bad?

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:28.480
No.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:29.440
No.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.880
It's an old name that's a bit playful.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:39.440
Humphrey Bogart was always a «private dick» and
it's ... It's an old word for «detective» ..

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:44.240
... that people just abbreviated. «I'm a private dick».

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:49.820
And you've stated that this is the first time
you can watch yourself on TV without feeling unwell?

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:51.440
Yes!

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:58.880
Yes! I think the character is so funny ... It's
a guy who thinks that he knows everything ...

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:00.240
... but he's so lost.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.560
Especially when it comes to digital stuff.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:05.600
He thinks that he has a clue, but he doesn't.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:10.000
But he's honest and he manages to accomplish
everything.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.240
I've got a small scene her, can we show it?

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:12.760
Yeah, yeah!

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:13.760
Have a look at this.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.160
Bring him back.

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:17.320
BRING HIM BACK!

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.220
He's a stubborn spirit, I can't!

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:20.560
I need something stronger!

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:21.900
Like what?

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.180
Well, okay, there are these candles.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:28.540
They're made out of Bolivian salt, and the
ancient Mayans used to use them, but they're ...

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:29.540
really expensive.

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:30.120
How much?

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:30.740
300.

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.480
Peter Stormare, thank you so much for being
here!

